  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "v5.8.15 - on SpawnDev.ILGPU 5.4.28. Much faster SHORT prompts on CUDA: qwen3:1.7b Q4_K_M on an RTX 4070 reaches the first token of a 29-token prompt at ~2,550 prompt tok/s with the default (5.8.14: ~930) and ~3,320 with the opt-in int8 mode (5.8.14: ~1,480; Ollama's own prompt-eval timer: ~3.4k). Three changes: (1) the batch decoder hands out a token read that has already completed before it queues the next step, so the first token no longer waits for the first decode step (env GGUF_HANDOUT_FIRST=0 is the A/B); (2) prompts of at most 64 tokens use new tensor-core GEMMs with a 32-row token tile (the 128-row tile computed mostly padding at short prompts; env GGUF_S8_M32_MAX / GGUF_KQ_M32_MAX / GGUF_F16_M32_MAX, 0 = off); (3) GEMMs whose grid is too small to fill the GPU split K into one wave of partial sums, reduced in a fixed order (deterministic; env GGUF_S8_SPLITK / GGUF_F16_SPLITK / GGUF_KQ_SPLITK = 0 are the A/Bs). Long prompts +2-3%, decode unchanged, the default path's numerics unchanged (the 32-row K-quant kernel is bit-identical to the 128-row one; split-K is held to the same error bound and was more accurate in the tests). FIX: Wan22Paths.InRepo() named a text encoder the models host does not serve (umt5 bf16, 404); it now defaults to the hosted 8-bit umT5, which is as faithful to the reference. Also: the int8 prefill GEMM spreads its per-tile scale work over all warps. See CHANGELOG.md.",
